 ## Overview
-X-ray projection (also called Forward Projection or aTx) is a volume rendering composition scheme where interpolated samples along a ray are simply summed to produce an image [S1]. It mimics the physical process of X-ray attenuation, where the total signal is the integral of density along the ray path [G]. Unlike alpha blending, which preserves depth cues through opacity accumulation, this method loses depth information, making it suitable for visualizing high-density structures like bones or vessels where relative depth is less critical than total integrated intensity [S1].
+X-ray projection (also called Forward Projection or aTx) is a volume rendering composition scheme where interpolated samples along a ray are simply summed to produce an image [S1]. It mimics the physical process of X-ray attenuation, where the total signal is the integral of density along the ray path [G]. Unlike alpha blending, which preserves depth cues through opacity accumulation, this method loses depth information, making it suitable for visualizing high-density structures like bones or vessels where relative depth is less critical than total integrated intensity [S1]. It builds on the shared compositing principles of [Projection and MIP Techniques](6305-projection-and-mip-techniques.md) but replaces the transmittance-weighted accumulation with a direct linear sum [G].
 
